Andrew Kerr says...
>  The problem is ensuring 
> that the client is approved by the organisers. We are not trying to 
> secure the IP socket itself.
> 
> And having phrased the problem like that, suddenly I see a better way: 
> CHAP, challenge handshake authentication protocol. Client-server NH 
> protocol remains plaintext.
> 
> The closed source clients and server know a passphrase. On connection, 
> the server sends a challenge including random salt. The client combines 
> the salt and passphrase, feeds the result through a hash function, and 
> sends the hash to the server. The server checks this against its own 
> calculation. If accepted, the server still challenges the client with a 
> different salt value at random intervals.

Um, a bot-client connects to the server, but passes challenges onto the 
authorised client. iptables could do the packet mangling required to 
fool the client. Would need a way to avoid that. Server combines game 
state with passphrase, and sends the hash of that as part of a 
challenge. The client can then check the game states are the same.

The bot-client could also read the game state from the IP socket, 
allowing the client to communicate directly with the server, and send 
commands into the client via the UI. Avoiding this would AFAICS require 
encrypting the socket. Negotiate a session key using asymmetric cypher, 
encrypt socket using symmetric cypher with session key. Keep challenges 
unencrypted to avoid known plaintext attacks, so keep the above game 
state plus passphrase hashing.

Damn it, this is hard :(

> It doesn't need to be unbreakable. It needs to be as hard to cheat as it 
> is now, since we already know that the current level of protection is 
> sufficient.

Well, actually we don't _know_ that no-one uses bots on the devnull 
server, but it does seem a reasonable assumption. At least no-one seems 
to have been successful using a bot.

I should point out that even if the client authentication system is 
unbreakable, it would still be possible to write a bot by parsing a tty 
output, in the way Farmbot does, and possibly reusing the Farmbot code.


